>> title: Japanese Rice
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: japanese, rice, staple, stovetop, side
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: ご飯, gohan, gohan, 白米, hakumai, haku mai, plain steamed rice, Japanese short-grain rice, sticky rice for a rice bowl
>> pairs-with: miso-soup
>> servings: 4
>> time: 55 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the ten-minute rest is the last of the cooking: lift the lid and the steam that finishes the top layer is gone

>> step: The stove version. The proportion is the same as a rice cooker's.
Most households cook this in a rice cooker, and the machine is better at it.

>> step: rinse until it runs clear
Rinse @short-grain rice{2%cups}(475 mL, 370 g; Japanese or Korean, never long grain) in @cold water{6%cups}(1.4 L; poured off and replaced four or five times) ~rinse{3%min}, swirling it with your fingers until the water runs almost clear instead of milky.

>> step: soak 30 min
Soak @&(~1)rinsed rice{} in @water{2 1/4%cups}(525 mL) in a #heavy saucepan{} ~soak{30%min}, until every grain has gone from translucent to opaque white right through.

>> step: boil, then 12 min on the lowest heat
Bring @&(~1)soaked rice{} to a boil uncovered over medium-high heat, then clamp on the lid and drop to the lowest flame the burner has ~steam{12%min}.

>> step: rest 10 min, lid on
Rest @&(~1)rice{} off the heat with the lid still shut ~rest{10%min}, then fold it over twice from the bottom with a wetted paddle.
